@*
	Who is just up the road and just behind, over the live map (§5.4).

	The one question a rider asks a group-ride app while actually moving. Everything else on
	this screen is read at a stop; this is read at 40 km/h out of the corner of an eye, which
	is what every decision below follows from.

	**Translucent panel, solid text.** The map is the page — a rider covering a strip of it
	with an opaque card has hidden the thing they came for, and the ground under this panel is
	behind them or ahead of them and worth seeing. The text is not translucent with it: a name
	at 55% over a satellite tile is unreadable, and a panel nobody can read is an opaque one
	that also failed. So the surface is see-through and the type on it is not.

	**It never takes a tap.** `pointer-events: none` on the whole thing, so a pan that starts
	on it reaches the base map like a pan starting anywhere else. It is a read-out, not a
	control; the way to be rid of it is the hamburger, which is what put it there.

	**Road order, leader at the top.** The rows are in the order the riders are actually in on
	the route, so a rider off the front finds themselves at the top of the panel and one off
	the back finds themselves at the bottom — the shape of it answers "am I ahead or behind"
	before a single number has been read. NeighbourList is what settles that; this is layout.

	**The swatch is never the whole of an answer.** It matches the rider's pin on the map
	(§16.3), which is what makes the panel and the map name the same person the same way — but
	colour through a visor in daylight is the first thing to go, so the name is beside it in
	words and the gap is in words too.
*@

@if (Rows.Count > 0)
{
	<aside class="live-neighbours" aria-label="Travellers nearest you along the route">
		<ul>
			@foreach (NeighbourRow row in Rows)
			{
				<li class="@(row.IsSelf ? "self" : null)">
					<span class="swatch" style="background: @row.Colour" aria-hidden="true"></span>
					<span class="name">@row.UserName</span>
					<span class="gap">
						@if (row.IsSelf)
						{
							@* The point everything else is measured from says so rather than
							   reporting "level" with itself, which is arithmetic rather than news. *@
							<span class="you">you</span>
						}
						else if (row.Presence == MemberPresence.NoSignal)
						{
							@*
								Their pin is still on the map and it stopped moving (§5.6). The number
								is still the best answer there is — it is where they were — but a gap
								that has quietly frozen is exactly the thing that must not be read as
								a gap that is holding steady, so it says which it is.
							*@
							<span class="stale" title="No signal — this is where they were">
								@NeighbourList.FormatRelative(row.RelativeMetres)
							</span>
						}
						else
						{
							@NeighbourList.FormatRelative(row.RelativeMetres)
						}
					</span>
				</li>
			}
		</ul>
	</aside>
}

@code {
	/// <summary>
	/// The riders to draw, road order, from <see cref="NeighbourList.Nearest"/>. An empty list
	/// renders nothing at all — a panel saying it has nothing to say is a panel covering the map
	/// for no reason.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<NeighbourRow> Rows { get; set; } = [];
}

<style>
	/*
		Down the left edge, vertically centred.

		Absolute inside the live page's .page-full rather than fixed, so it lands inside the
		content area on both rail placements without restating the rail's geometry — the same
		arrangement .chrome uses on the opposite edge.

		The left edge is the one that is free. The top-right carries the hamburger and the two
		camera-mode buttons, the top-left the base map's compass, the bottom the placing strip
		and the mode toast. Centring vertically keeps a five-row panel clear of the compass above it and of
		the strips below, at every phone height, without any of them having to know it is here.

		--dlr-safe-left and not env(safe-area-inset-left): iOS reports a side inset on BOTH edges
		in landscape whichever edge the cutout is on, and the raw value would float this a
		cutout's width in from an edge that has no cutout (app.css).
	*/
	.live-neighbours {
		position: absolute;
		top: 50%;
		left: max(var(--dlr-map-control-inset), var(--dlr-safe-left));
		transform: translateY(-50%);
		/* Under the menu backdrop (19) and the strips (18), over the map. Nothing here is
		   interactive, so being covered costs the rider nothing. */
		z-index: 16;
		/* A read-out, not a control: a pan that starts here reaches the map. */
		pointer-events: none;
		width: max-content;
		max-width: min(13rem, 45vw);
		padding: 0.35rem 0.5rem;
		/* See-through to the ground, because the map is the page. The border is what keeps the
		   panel's shape legible over a light tile — over dark ones the fill does it. */
		background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.55);
		border: 1px solid rgba(226, 232, 240, 0.2);
		border-radius: 8px;
		box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.35);
		/* Where it is supported, this is what lets the fill stay this light: the tiles under the
		   panel stop competing with the type. Where it is not, the fill alone still carries it. */
		backdrop-filter: blur(2px);
	}

	.live-neighbours ul {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
	}

	.live-neighbours li {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.4rem;
		padding: 0.12rem 0;
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		/* Solid, over a surface that is not. The shadow is what carries a name across the seam
		   between a dark tile and a bright one without the fill having to be opaque. */
		color: var(--dlr-fg);
		text-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,0.9);
	}

	/*
		The reader's own row. A left edge and a weight rather than a background — the same
		treatment the members list gives it — because a filled row on a panel this small reads as
		a selection, and nobody selected anything.
	*/
	.live-neighbours li.self {
		font-weight: 700;
		border-left: 3px solid var(--dlr-accent);
		margin-left: -0.5rem;
		padding-left: calc(0.5rem - 3px);
	}

	/* The colour their pin is drawn in (§16.3). Bordered, so a rider whose colour is close to
	   the tiles underneath still has a square rather than a smudge. */
	.live-neighbours .swatch {
		flex: 0 0 auto;
		width: 0.85rem;
		height: 0.85rem;
		border: 1px solid #000000a0;
		border-radius: 3px;
	}

	.live-neighbours .name {
		flex: 1 1 auto;
		min-width: 0;
		overflow: hidden;
		text-overflow: ellipsis;
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	/* Tabular figures: the numbers change every few seconds, and a column that reflowed as
	   "990 m" became "1.0 km" would be unreadable at the speed this is read at. */
	.live-neighbours .gap {
		flex: 0 0 auto;
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
	}

	.live-neighbours .you {
		color: var(--dlr-accent-fg);
		opacity: 0.75;
	}

	/* A frozen gap, marked as frozen (§5.6). */
	.live-neighbours .stale {
		color: var(--dlr-error);
	}
</style>
